#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac8f4d9c-772d-5eaf-8c84-65a14540efc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295085} {:answer/id #uuid "44287c21-953d-5109-a766-6d380fc57e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8f4d9c-772d-5eaf-8c84-65a14540efc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295085} :answer-field/id #uuid "03229dae-06ba-5e29-8fbb-8ef6b008118b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9fbc9f-0d9a-5a6a-bb39-ccd37eef6380" :question/math-academy-id "q-348330" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+2)}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x(x+2) & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+[{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}]) \\\\  & =-((x+1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+2)}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+2)}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arcsin\\,(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7528f7bc-4fc9-56b4-b4b9-fe004e499175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7528f7bc-4fc9-56b4-b4b9-fe004e499175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295089} :answer-field/id #uuid "b29cae2c-f5bc-56d9-8898-040d62e4827b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a269d9e0-82bb-5108-9963-a5e2675f46f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-348531" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{8}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+24 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+({5}^{2}-{5}^{2})+24 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2})-25+24 \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{8}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x=8{\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{1}{|x-5|\\sqrt{(x-5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-5).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $5+\\sqrt{2}$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{8}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =8(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fdbda33-28ac-50df-b7e7-34a8d62956a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295092} {:answer/id #uuid "3d85d71c-4c39-5764-928d-5118607aafea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fdbda33-28ac-50df-b7e7-34a8d62956a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295092} :answer-field/id #uuid "8547dfbe-df70-50df-8630-5792f0b1e927" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30dd410e-7aef-5a70-a99e-28bcdd79205a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348563" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-7}^{\\sqrt{3}-6}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+12x+37}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+37 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+37 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2})-36+37 \\\\  & =(x+6)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+12x+37}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x+6)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+6).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-7$ | $\\sqrt{3}-6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{\\sqrt{3}-6}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+12x+37}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e155305-45d4-59a8-a197-cca1c98952a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e155305-45d4-59a8-a197-cca1c98952a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295096} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f04565-56e0-5c4e-9323-a7a21d3ea346" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4ed1612-16df-51eb-a06c-632a835998ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-348572" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{5}\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+17 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+17 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+17 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{(x-4)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f1a0cf-f5bc-5b79-a021-f224c6d4818c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295099} {:answer/id #uuid "42f79024-4a1c-5153-9a73-6aea3c18098c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295100} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab3d4de-0592-5233-b318-0d057c8c5199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295101} {:answer/id #uuid "be4d742c-fe43-5592-abd4-e5bb212e19fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295102} {:answer/id #uuid "57751800-58a0-5428-8577-21054c9cf080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+4}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f1a0cf-f5bc-5b79-a021-f224c6d4818c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295099} :answer-field/id #uuid "90cd0988-c91f-5f73-9855-e9d272b4ddfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42e9a6d9-ad48-538a-b628-daba76291c59" :question/math-academy-id "q-49655" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7+6x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+6x-{x}^{2} & =-({x}^{2}-6x-7) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+[{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}]-7) \\\\  & =-((x-3)^{2}-16) \\\\  & =16-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7+6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-(x-3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16(1-(x-3)^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x-3)^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{1-(x-3)^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x-3)/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-3}{4}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-3}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7+6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x-3)/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
